::: {.cell-output-display}
![Changes (days/year) in predicted dates of first adult emergence for STB across the CONUS over the entire 44-year period (1981–2025). Maps show (A) all changes and (B) statistically significant changes (P < 0.1); areas shown in gray are not statistically significant.](./STB/figure1.png){#fig-stb-emergence fig-align='center' width=70%}
:::
:::



::: {.cell layout-align="center"}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Changes (days/year) in predicted dates of first egg hatch for STB across the CONUS over the entire 44-year period (1981–2025). Maps show (A) all changes and (B) statistically significant changes (P < 0.1); areas shown in gray are not statistically significant.](./STB/figure2.png){#fig-stb-egg-hatch fig-align='center' width=70%}
:::
:::


#### **Recent period: 2001--2025**

During 2001–2025, adult emergence and egg hatch showed a stronger tendency toward later dates across portions of the northern and central United States, with earlier events remaining in scattered western and southern areas (@fig-stb-emergence-recent, @fig-stb-egg-hatch-recent). Significant changes were relatively localized, with some concentrated areas in the central United States.


